WEBVTT

00:00:00.430 --> 00:00:03.700
Begin with one runtime that makes the first useful workload easy.

00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:07.695
LM Studio provides a desktop interface for discovering compatible models,

00:00:07.695 --> 00:00:10.278
adjusting settings, chatting, and exposing a local API.

00:00:10.278 --> 00:00:14.165
Ollama emphasizes a simple command-line and service workflow with packaged model

00:00:14.165 --> 00:00:14.945
definitions.

00:00:14.945 --> 00:00:18.900
Both can reduce setup friction and help confirm that the hardware and driver stack work

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:22.725
before additional components are introduced. Llama.cpp is a foundational,

00:00:22.725 --> 00:00:27.015
highly portable inference project with extensive control over quantized models,

00:00:27.015 --> 00:00:29.310
CPU execution, and several GPU backends.

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:33.540
It is useful when you need explicit tuning or want to understand the underlying inference

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:34.080
options.

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:38.436
Open WebUI adds a browser-based conversation layer and can connect to backends such as

00:00:38.436 --> 00:00:40.930
Ollama or compatible local API servers.

00:00:40.930 --> 00:00:44.410
Text-generation-webui offers a broad set of loaders and controls,

00:00:44.410 --> 00:00:47.440
but its flexibility also creates more configuration choices.

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:50.210
Select a tool because it supports a defined use case,

00:00:50.210 --> 00:00:52.950
not because it exposes the largest number of switches.

00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:56.120
Local API servers allow editors, scripts, retrieval systems,

00:00:56.120 --> 00:00:59.160
and other applications to call the model through a consistent interface.

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:03.380
Start by binding the service to the loopback address so only the workstation can reach it.

00:01:03.380 --> 00:01:07.780
Test a single client, confirm authentication options if remote access is later required,

00:01:07.780 --> 00:01:11.640
and document the model name, port, context settings, and startup method.

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:14.360
Avoid unnecessary orchestration during the first pass.

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:16.760
Multiple front ends, containers, proxy layers,

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:19.480
and model servers make failures harder to isolate.

00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:23.480
Establish a simple path from model file to successful accelerated response.

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:25.655
Once that path is measured and repeatable,

00:01:25.655 --> 00:01:28.300
add interfaces or automation one component at a time.

00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:31.100
Simplicity at the beginning is not a limitation;

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:33.600
it is a controlled baseline for everything that follows.
